                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "VIS Games are no newcomers to releasing sub-par titles; their most well known 'works of art' being Obscuritas and the two Pineview Drive titles. The majority of the rest are mediocre simulator games. After taking a break from horror, they're now back with TheNightfall, which is essentially a Pineview Drive clone with a new skin and equally as enigmatic gameplay. With that being said, before you even dive into this review you should enter this realm with exceedingly low expectations, given the past that we're dealing with.\n\nYou play as a woman named Victoria who has moved to a new city, started a new job, and purchased a new house. She arrives at her empty home, to a voicemail left by her husband; attempting to reach him, she notices that the phone line in her house has been cut. From here on out, every hour (and in some cases, half-hour) is an experience in note-finding and event-triggering. Victoria must survive from 8pm until 6am, all the while uncovering the secrets of the house. The story seems to revolve around some John Wayne Gacy type who dressed up as a clown and murdered little children.\n\nSo yeah, you run around the house and trigger events. That's basically it. You have a flashlight that's totally useless if you turn up the brightness of the game; it's better than working on hoarding batteries and dealing with the fact that the lamp is always pointed at the ground. You can find matches and candles, but those are also pretty useless. You run in and out of each room, opening all of the drawers and cupboards, waiting until you hear the clock chime another hour away, and then you look for the note that has spawned. TheNightfall is really just as boring as it sounds. Even more disappointing is the fact that there is no horror in this game. Sometimes the events that occur will have clowns, dolls, an icecream truck, or spooky moving objects; no more, no less.\n\nHalf the time it's a crapshoot as to what you're supposed to do next. I'm now stuck at 2:30am after chasing around an icecream truck for the better part of an hour, trying to figure out what to do. Seems like many folks in the forums have gotten stuck at this time, and at 5:30am. There's absolutely no gameplay direction; not in the dialogue or notes, and nowhere else. For now, the Let's Players of the world have yet to make it this far, and there are no detailed walkthroughs available.\n\nSuffice it to say, after the two Pineview Drive titles and Obscuritas, VIS Games have done absolutely nothing to better the games that they create. The storytelling is still excruciatingly painful, there is no gameplay direction, and the whole experience feels needlessly convoluted. You would think with all of the feedback that they received that they would've at least taken something away from their past mistakes. There is only one improvement here over their previous releases, and that is optimization. I pretty well tore Obscuritas apart in my review after only getting 20 FPS on all low settings with an i7-6700k/GTX 1080 rig. Thankfully, TheNightfall at least runs at a stable 60 FPS, and it also never crashed. Unfortunately, even for free, I never actually had fun playing TheNightfall, and it surely isn't worth the $28.99 CAD pricetag, either. Hopefully VISGames' gameplay formula will change in the future.\n\n[h1]Rating: [b]1.0/5.0[/b] - It's Bad.[/h1]\nThe Horror Network [url=http://store.steampowered.com/curator/28221963/]Curator[/url] | [url=http://steamcommunity.com/groups/thehorrornetwork]Group[/url] Click for Gore\n\n[b]The developer provided a free copy of this game for review.[/b]",
